<Application xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.App">

  <!--
    Four style layers, in this order:

      1. FluentTheme        — the standard control set (settings window, message box): tabs,
                              dropdowns, checkboxes, text boxes, scrollbars, focus, IME. Avalonia's
                              own theme is Fluent 1 vocabulary, which layers 2 and 3 re-proportion.
      2. Aero/AeroResources — the vendored Windows 7 Aero colour layer: 168 <Color> keys per variant
                              and the 110 brushes built from them, plus the metrics. Every key in it
                              is Aero-prefixed, and as of Round 7 Task 2 NOTHING references any of
                              them — it is here so that the next task's first reference is a build
                              error if the layer is wrong, rather than a silently odd-looking app.
                              That file is the authority on its own contents and on why it sits at
                              this position in the list.
      3. Fluent2/Tokens     — TanMenu's design tokens, Light + Dark, plus the Aero caption brushes
                              MainWindow's replaced WindowDrawnDecorations template reads. Also
                              overrides the three FluentTheme keys this app redefines for Avalonia's
                              own templates: ControlCornerRadius, OverlayCornerRadius and
                              ContentControlThemeFontFamily, which pull the standard controls into
                              this app's proportions and typography. That seam works because this
                              file is listed AFTER FluentTheme below and Application.Styles is
                              searched last-first — Tokens.axaml is the authority on it and carries
                              the measurements; do not re-derive it here.
      4. Fluent2/Launcher   — the launcher's own dialect. Every rule is an x:Key'd ControlTheme or a
                              .tm-* class selector, so it is opt-in and cannot leak into the
                              settings window or the message box.

    THE ORDER OF 2 AND 3 IS THE POINT, not incidental. Application.Styles is searched last-first
    (Avalonia.Styling.Styles.TryGetResource counts DOWN from Count-1), so a key defined in a LATER
    entry wins. Aero after Fluent means Aero beats Fluent; Tokens after Aero means this app's own
    overrides still beat Aero. ContentControlThemeFontFamily is the one that would bite: the
    upstream Aero metrics file set it to a bundled font with no CJK coverage, which would render
    this app's Chinese item names as tofu. That line is deleted on vendoring AND this ordering keeps
    Tokens' value on top of it.

    RequestedThemeVariant is deliberately NOT pinned here any more. The retro theme was a fixed
    light palette, so the old build had to force Light or the OS could put dark Fluent chrome around
    a light launcher. Both the token layer and the vendored Aero layer have both variants, so the
    variant is a user setting (general.appearance) applied at startup by Services/AppTheme.
  -->
  <Application.Styles>
    <FluentTheme />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Aero/AeroResources.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Fluent2/Tokens.axaml" />
    <StyleInclude Source="avares://TanMenu.Wpf/Themes/Fluent2/Launcher.axaml" />
  </Application.Styles>

</Application>
